  "decisions": {
    "type qualifiers": "U1 #typecasting, not U4 #storage-classes-in-c. The question asks which keyword makes a variable unchangeable; the answer is `const`, which is a CONSTANT in syllabus Unit 1 (Basics of C) and is taught there (15 mentions in unit-1). Unit 4's heading mentions 'Qualifiers' but its body is a storage-class table (auto/register/static/extern) with no `const` or `volatile` teaching, so it is a heading-level match with no body. Chose the unit that actually teaches the answer.",
    "comments / conditionals / modular programming": "U4 #modular-programming-and-functions, not U1 #headers-and-library-files. The topic names three sub-topics, and the 8-mark question splits 2+3+3 across them: (a) comments (2m), (b) if-else (3m), (c) modular programming and how functions help (3m). Modular programming is the only sub-topic with a dedicated, fully-named section, and it carries the equal largest share of marks. The previous 'most specific sub-topic' rule was too crude -- it picked 'comments' without looking at marks. Amended rule: weigh marks."
